---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import Katex from '../../components/Katex.astro';
import '../../styles/paper.css';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';

// Rewritten against the published Nature Biotechnology paper (PMC11180609). The page previously
// summarised the 2022 bioRxiv preprint and carried performance numbers -- MSE 0.65/0.38,
// precision 0.42/0.62, r = 0.81, "284 2-gene combinations" -- that the published paper does not
// contain. Checked by `node scripts/verify-paper-claims.mjs --slug gears`.
const title = "Predicting Transcriptional Outcomes of Novel Multigene Perturbations with GEARS";
const description = "Technical summary of GEARS (Roohani, Huang & Leskovec, Nature Biotechnology 2024): a graph neural network over a gene-ontology knowledge graph that predicts Perturb-seq outcomes for gene combinations never experimentally perturbed.";
const readingTime = "22 min read";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} type="article" noindex={true}>
  <article class="paper-article">
    <nav style="margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
      <a href="/papers/" style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; font-size: var(--fs-small); color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600;">
        <Icon name="arrow-left" size={14} /> Back to Paper Summaries
      </a>
    </nav>

    <header class="paper-header">
      <div class="paper-meta-bar">
        <span class="paper-tag">Perturbation Modelling & Graph Neural Networks</span>
        <span class="paper-venue-badge">Nature Biotechnology · June 2024</span>
        <span class="paper-time-badge"><Icon name="clock" size={14} /> {readingTime}</span>
      </div>

      <h1 class="paper-title">{title}</h1>

      <div class="paper-authors-box">
        <div><strong>Authors:</strong> Yusuf Roohani, Kexin Huang, Jure Leskovec</div>
        <div><strong>Affiliation:</strong> Department of Computer Science, Stanford University, Stanford, CA</div>
        <div><strong>DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41587-023-01905-6" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: var(--color-accent);">10.1038/s41587-023-01905-6</a> · Nature Biotechnology 42, 927–935 (received 9 July 2022, accepted 12 July 2023)</div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <p class="paper-lead">
      The number of possible multigene perturbations explodes combinatorially, so the interesting experiments
      are exactly the ones nobody can run. <strong>GEARS</strong> — graph-enhanced gene activation and
      repression simulator — predicts the full post-perturbation transcriptome for combinations
      <strong>consisting of genes that were never experimentally perturbed</strong>, by giving a graph neural
      network a <strong>knowledge graph of gene–gene relationships</strong> to generalise over. It achieves
      <strong>40% higher precision</strong> than existing approaches across four genetic-interaction subtypes,
      with improvement <strong>exceeding 90% for redundancy and epistasis</strong>, and identifies the
      strongest interactions <strong>twice as well</strong> as prior methods.
    </p>

    <div class="paper-toc">
      <div class="paper-toc-title">
        <Icon name="list" size={14} /> Table of Contents
      </div>
      <ol class="paper-toc-list">
        <li><a href="#summary">1. The Combinatorial Explosion Problem</a></li>
        <li><a href="#architecture">2. Architecture: Two Graphs and a Gene-Specific Decoder</a></li>
        <li><a href="#generalization">3. The Generalization Classes That Define the Task</a></li>
        <li><a href="#results">4. Results: Expression Prediction</a></li>
        <li><a href="#genetic-interactions">5. Genetic Interaction Subtypes</a></li>
        <li><a href="#uncertainty">6. Bayesian Uncertainty and the Ablations</a></li>
        <li><a href="#mathematical-formulation">7. Mathematical Formulation</a></li>
        <li><a href="#failure-modes">8. Limitations & What the Predictions Support</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <section id="summary" class="paper-section">
      <h2>1. The Combinatorial Explosion Problem</h2>
      <p>
        Perturb-seq pairs a pooled CRISPR screen with a single-cell RNA-seq readout, so each perturbed cell
        reports the entire transcriptome. Single-gene screens are now routine — the Replogle et al. datasets
        used here each measure <strong>over 170,000 cells</strong>. Pairs are not: with <em>n</em> genes there
        are <Katex tex="\binom{n}{2}" /> combinations, and the screen that would answer the question cannot be
        built.
      </p>
      <p>
        The paper's motivation is concrete — identifying genetic interactions in cancer, and finding
        synergistic gene pairs to make combination therapies more effective. Both require knowing which pairs
        are worth testing before testing them.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-callout callout-info">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="target" size={15} /> Why the naive baseline is the one to beat
        </div>
        <p>
          If the effects of perturbing two genes separately are known, the obvious prediction for perturbing
          both is to add them. That additive model is not a straw man — it is right whenever the genes act
          independently, which is most of the time. Every interesting case is a departure from it, and the
          paper restricts its error analysis to the <strong>top 20 most differentially expressed genes</strong>
          precisely so that performance is measured where the departure lives rather than diluted across a
          transcriptome of mostly unchanged genes.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="architecture" class="paper-section">
      <h2>2. Architecture: Two Graphs and a Gene-Specific Decoder</h2>

      <h3>The perturbation similarity graph</h3>
      <p>
        GEARS predicts the outcome of perturbing genes <strong>never seen perturbed before</strong> by
        constructing a <strong>gene perturbation similarity graph</strong> from the Gene Ontology. Two genes
        are connected according to how many GO pathways they share — the <strong>Jaccard index</strong> over
        their pathway sets. A gene with no perturbation data of its own inherits an inductive bias from the
        genes it shares pathways with, which is the entire mechanism by which the model can say anything about
        an unseen perturbation.
      </p>

      <h3>The co-expression graph</h3>
      <p>
        A second graph relates genes by co-expression, and operates on the output side: the decoder propagates
        a perturbation's effect across genes that move together. The ablations treat these as separable
        components — <em>No GO Graph</em> and <em>No Co-Expression Graph</em> are tested independently — which
        is how the paper shows each contributes.
      </p>

      <h3>The decoder</h3>
      <p>
        Rather than a single shared output head, GEARS uses a <strong>gene-specific decoder MLP</strong> plus a
        <strong>cross-gene MLP layer</strong>. The ablation replacing the gene-specific decoder with a shared
        one is reported separately, and the distinction matters: a shared decoder forces every gene to respond
        to the perturbation embedding in the same functional form.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="generalization" class="paper-section">
      <h2>3. The Generalization Classes That Define the Task</h2>
      <p>
        For two-gene perturbations the paper defines three classes by how many of the two genes were seen
        experimentally perturbed during training. This taxonomy is what makes the results interpretable —
        aggregate accuracy over a mixed test set would hide exactly the case the method exists for.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Class</th>
              <th>Genes seen perturbed individually in training</th>
              <th>Difficulty</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>2/2 seen</strong></td>
              <td>Both</td>
              <td>Easiest — the additive baseline is available and often adequate</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>1/2 unseen</strong></td>
              <td>One</td>
              <td>Half the additive prediction is unavailable</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>0/2 unseen</strong></td>
              <td>Neither</td>
              <td>Hardest — no direct evidence about either gene; only the graph</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        GEARS improves mean squared error <strong>by more than 30% across all cases</strong>, and the
        <strong>largest improvement, 53%, occurs when both perturbed genes are unseen</strong> — the class
        where an additive model has nothing to add. The gradient across classes is the result: the harder the
        generalization, the more the knowledge graph is worth.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="results" class="paper-section">
      <h2>4. Results: Expression Prediction</h2>
      <p>
        Performance is measured as mean squared error and Pearson correlation between predicted and true
        post-perturbation expression on held-out perturbations, restricted to the top 20 most differentially
        expressed genes. GEARS was trained separately on each dataset.
      </p>
      <p>
        Beyond error, the paper reports <strong>50% greater enrichment for the most significant differentially
        expressed genes</strong> as predicted by GEARS than by the comparison. This is the practically relevant
        metric: an experimentalist choosing which perturbation to run cares whether the model surfaces the
        right genes, not whether its error is small on genes that barely move.
      </p>
      <p>
        The worked example given is <em>FOSB</em> with <em>CEBPB</em>, where GEARS captured
        <strong>both the right trend and the magnitude</strong> of the perturbation across all 20
        differentially expressed genes.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="genetic-interactions" class="paper-section">
      <h2>5. Genetic Interaction Subtypes</h2>
      <p>
        A genetic interaction is any departure from the additive expectation, and the departures have distinct
        shapes. GEARS predicts post-perturbation expression for all combinations, then computes GI scores from
        the predictions and classifies them into five subtypes: <strong>synergy, suppression, neomorphism,
        epistasis and redundancy</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Result</th>
              <th>Value</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Precision@10 improvement over baselines</td>
              <td><strong>&gt;40% for four of five</strong> interaction subtypes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Improvement for redundancy and epistasis</td>
              <td><strong>&gt;90%</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Identification of the strongest interactions</td>
              <td><strong>Twice as well</strong> as existing approaches</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Evaluation set</td>
              <td><strong>131 two-gene combinations</strong> (Norman et al. Perturb-seq)</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>Scaling to a screen that was never run</h3>
      <p>
        The demonstration that makes the case is exhaustive prediction over <strong>all 5,151 pairwise
        combinations of 102 genes</strong>, following a CRISPRa-based combinatorial gene activation design.
        GEARS recovers clusters previously identified in Norman et al. and predicts a few new phenotypes —
        which is what a screen-prioritisation tool has to do: reproduce the known structure and then propose
        something outside it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="uncertainty" class="paper-section">
      <h2>6. Bayesian Uncertainty and the Ablations</h2>
      <p>
        A model asked about perturbations it has never seen must be able to say when it does not know. GEARS
        uses a <strong>Bayesian formulation with a Gaussian likelihood</strong> to output an
        <strong>uncertainty score that is inversely correlated with model performance</strong> — high
        uncertainty where the prediction is poor. For screen prioritisation this is arguably as important as
        accuracy, since it converts a ranked list into a ranked list with a confidence column.
      </p>
      <p>
        The ablations remove one component at a time — <em>No Graph</em>, <em>No GO Graph</em>, <em>No
        Co-Expression Graph</em>, <em>No Cross-gene MLP</em>, <em>No Gene-specific Decoder</em>, and
        <em>MSE Loss</em> in place of the auto-focus loss — and are evaluated across all four generalization
        conditions rather than in aggregate.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="mathematical-formulation" class="paper-section">
      <h2>7. Mathematical Formulation</h2>

      <h3>Gene similarity from the ontology</h3>
      <p>
        For genes <Katex tex="u" /> and <Katex tex="v" /> with GO pathway sets
        <Katex tex="\mathcal{N}_u" /> and <Katex tex="\mathcal{N}_v" />, edge weight is the Jaccard index:
      </p>
      <div class="paper-equation-block">
        <Katex tex="J_{u,v} = \frac{|\mathcal{N}_u \cap \mathcal{N}_v|}{|\mathcal{N}_u \cup \mathcal{N}_v|}" />
      </div>

      <h3>The direction loss</h3>
      <p>
        Alongside the auto-focus reconstruction loss, GEARS penalises predicting the wrong <em>sign</em> of
        change relative to control — over perturbations <Katex tex="k" />, cells <Katex tex="l" /> and genes
        <Katex tex="u" />:
      </p>
      <div class="paper-equation-block">
        <Katex tex="\mathcal{L}_{\text{direction}} = \frac{1}{T}\sum_{k=1}^{T}\frac{1}{T_k}\sum_{l=1}^{T_k}\sum_{u=1}^{G}\left[\operatorname{sign}\!\left(g_u - g_u^{\text{ctrl}}\right) - \operatorname{sign}\!\left(\hat{g}_u - g_u^{\text{ctrl}}\right)\right]" />
      </div>
      <p>
        Separating direction from magnitude is a deliberate choice. Squared error alone is happy to
        under-predict an upregulation rather than risk predicting a downregulation, and for a biologist
        deciding whether a pair activates or represses a program, the sign is the answer.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="failure-modes" class="paper-section">
      <h2>8. Limitations & What the Predictions Support</h2>

      <h3>The knowledge graph is the ceiling</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Generalization runs through the Gene Ontology.</strong> A gene poorly annotated in GO has few informative neighbours, so the inductive bias that carries unseen perturbations is weakest exactly for under-studied genes — the ones most worth screening. The paper notes that other knowledge graphs, such as context-specific networks, may suit different gene sets better.</li>
        <li><strong>GO is itself literature-derived.</strong> Predictions therefore inherit the field's existing attention, which is a circularity worth naming when the output is used to decide what to study next.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Scope of the experimental setting</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>One cell type per trained model.</strong> GEARS was trained separately on each dataset; nothing here claims transfer across cellular contexts, and a genetic interaction is a property of a context as much as of a gene pair.</li>
        <li><strong>Transcriptional readout only.</strong> The prediction is post-perturbation mRNA. Protein-level, metabolic and phenotypic consequences are outside the model.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>What a prediction licenses</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Prioritisation, not substitution.</strong> Twice as good at finding the strongest interactions is a statement about ranking a candidate list for experimental follow-up. The uncertainty score exists because the authors expect the list to be wrong somewhere.</li>
        <li><strong>Subtype calls are computed from predicted expression</strong>, so a classification into synergy or epistasis inherits the expression model's error and should be read as a hypothesis about the interaction's shape.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="paper-callout callout-info">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="book" size={15} /> Source note
        </div>
        <p>
          Written against the published Nature Biotechnology article. An earlier version of this page was
          based on the 2022 bioRxiv preprint and quoted performance figures that do not appear in the
          published paper; those have been replaced with the reported results above.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
